{"assessment":"The source provides a perturbative asymptotic series for the inverse localization length in the 1D Anderson model with stealthy hyperuniform disorder. The selected cluster is the leading-order scaling family: for chi=0.1 the first nonzero Born order is W^2 giving xi ~ W^{-2}, and for chi=0.2 the second nonzero order is W^4 giving xi ~ W^{-4}. The routing hint directs extraction of the normalized leading localization-length predictor Y(q)=lim_{W_ref->0 at fixed q} xi(q*W_ref)/xi(W_ref)=q^{-p}, which cancels all unreported chi-dependent coefficients and avoids absolute W domains and figure data. The source supports the exponents p=2 (first order, chi=0.1) and p=4 (second order, chi=0.2) via the perturbation theory statements and the explicit second-order expression. The normalized predictor is a theoretical leading asymptotic quantity, not a finite-size numerical xi. The source does not provide a continuous W domain for the absolute xi, so the control is the finite benchmark ratio q in [1,10] within the fixed-ratio asymptotic variable. The private branches are chi=0.1 and chi=0.2, each with distinct source-anchored fixed conditions. The ASTs are identical power(q, negative_p) with private exponents -2 and -4. No absolute coefficients, E, k0, S(2k), or numerical protocol are encoded in the oracle. The source_ambiguities array is empty because the selected cluster is unambiguous. case_authoring_ready is true.","case_authoring_ready":true,"claims":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":260,"line_start":260,"support":"Equation (eq:first-order) gives 1/xi = W^2 S(2k)/(8 sin^2 k), so xi ~ W^{-2}."},{"line_end":352,"line_start":352,"support":"Text states for chi=0.1, perturbation theory predicts xi ~ W^{-2}."}],"applicability":"Valid for chi=0.1, energy E=-1.8, and weak disorder W << 1. The first-order term is nonzero because S(2k) is nonzero for this chi.","claim_id":"first_order_normalized_scaling_chi_0_1","control_domains":[{"allowed_values":[],"anchors":[{"line_end":128,"line_start":128,"support":"Perturbation theory at small disorder W << 1, which justifies the fixed-ratio asymptotic limit."},{"line_end":316,"line_start":312,"support":"The inverse localization length is expressed as an asymptotic series in powers of W^2, supporting the scaling form."}],"control_name":"benchmark_ratio_q","domain_kind":"continuous_interval","domain_statement":"Finite benchmark ratio q in [1,10] within the fixed-ratio asymptotic variable, as specified by the routing hint.","maximum":10.0,"minimum":1.0}],"control_names":["benchmark_ratio_q"],"depends_on":[],"fixed_condition_ids":["stealthiness_chi_0_1","energy_E_minus_1_8","hopping_amplitude_unity"],"mechanism_ids":["first_order_self_energy"],"oracle_outputs":{"normalized_localization_length_predictor":{"args":[{"var":"benchmark_ratio_q"},{"param":"first_order_exponent"}],"op":"power"}},"quantitative_form":"Y(q) = q^{-2}","readout_names":["normalized_localization_length_predictor"],"statement":"For chi=0.1 and energy E=-1.8, the leading-order localization length scales as xi ~ W^{-2}. The normalized predictor Y(q) = q^{-2}.","statistical_basis":"Theoretical leading asymptotic prediction; no numerical averaging or fitting is involved.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified"},{"anchors":[{"line_end":280,"line_start":280,"support":"Text states the correction to xi at second order will be of the form xi ~ W^{-4}."},{"line_end":352,"line_start":352,"support":"Text states for chi=0.2, perturbation theory predicts xi ~ W^{-4}."}],"applicability":"Valid for chi=0.2, energy E=-1.8, and weak disorder W << 1. The first-order term vanishes because S(2k)=0 for this chi.","claim_id":"second_order_normalized_scaling_chi_0_2","control_domains":[{"allowed_values":[],"anchors":[{"line_end":128,"line_start":128,"support":"Perturbation theory at small disorder W << 1, which justifies the fixed-ratio asymptotic limit."},{"line_end":316,"line_start":312,"support":"The inverse localization length is expressed as an asymptotic series in powers of W^2, supporting the scaling form."}],"control_name":"benchmark_ratio_q","domain_kind":"continuous_interval","domain_statement":"Finite benchmark ratio q in [1,10] within the fixed-ratio asymptotic variable, as specified by the routing hint.","maximum":10.0,"minimum":1.0}],"control_names":["benchmark_ratio_q"],"depends_on":[],"fixed_condition_ids":["stealthiness_chi_0_2","energy_E_minus_1_8","hopping_amplitude_unity"],"mechanism_ids":["second_order_self_energy"],"oracle_outputs":{"normalized_localization_length_predictor":{"args":[{"var":"benchmark_ratio_q"},{"param":"second_order_exponent"}],"op":"power"}},"quantitative_form":"Y(q) = q^{-4}","readout_names":["normalized_localization_length_predictor"],"statement":"For chi=0.2 and energy E=-1.8, the leading-order localization length scales as xi ~ W^{-4}. The normalized predictor Y(q) = q^{-4}.","statistical_basis":"Theoretical leading asymptotic prediction; no numerical averaging or fitting is involved.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified"}],"controls":[{"allowed_values":[],"anchors":[{"line_end":128,"line_start":128,"support":"Perturbation theory at small disorder W << 1, which justifies the fixed-ratio asymptotic limit."},{"line_end":316,"line_start":312,"support":"The inverse localization length is expressed as an asymptotic series in powers of W^2, supporting the scaling form."}],"definition":"Finite benchmark ratio q = W/W_ref, used in the fixed-ratio asymptotic variable. The normalized predictor is Y(q) = lim_{W_ref->0 at fixed q} xi(q*W_ref)/xi(W_ref) = q^{-p}.","domain_kind":"continuous_interval","domain_statement":"Finite benchmark ratio q in [1,10] within the fixed-ratio asymptotic variable, as specified by the routing hint. The source does not provide a continuous W domain for absolute xi, so the control is the ratio q.","maximum":10.0,"minimum":1.0,"name":"benchmark_ratio_q","unit":"dimensionless"}],"fixed_conditions":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":115,"line_start":115,"support":"Text defines chi = k0/(2*pi)."},{"line_end":240,"line_start":240,"support":"Text states chi = k0/(2*pi) and S(q) = Theta(|q|-k0)."},{"line_end":265,"line_start":265,"support":"Text states if k0 > 2k, the first-order term vanishes; for chi=0.1, k0=0.2*pi and at E=-1.8, k is such that k0 < 2k, so S(2k) is nonzero."},{"line_end":345,"line_start":345,"support":"Caption of Fig. loclength_W lists chi=0.1 as a value for which perturbation theory predicts xi ~ W^{-2}."},{"line_end":352,"line_start":352,"support":"Text states for chi=0.1, perturbation theory predicts xi ~ W^{-2}."}],"condition_id":"stealthiness_chi_0_1","statement":"Stealthiness parameter chi = 0.1, corresponding to k0 = 2*pi*chi = 0.2*pi. For this chi, the first-order term is nonzero because S(2k) is nonzero."},{"anchors":[{"line_end":115,"line_start":115,"support":"Text defines chi = k0/(2*pi)."},{"line_end":240,"line_start":240,"support":"Text states chi = k0/(2*pi) and S(q) = Theta(|q|-k0)."},{"line_end":265,"line_start":265,"support":"Text states if k0 > 2k, the first-order term vanishes; for chi=0.2, k0=0.4*pi and at E=-1.8, k is such that 2k < k0, so S(2k)=0."},{"line_end":280,"line_start":280,"support":"Text states the second-order correction is of the form xi ~ W^{-4} when 2k < k0 < pi - k."},{"line_end":345,"line_start":345,"support":"Caption of Fig. loclength_W lists chi=0.2 as a value for which perturbation theory predicts xi ~ W^{-4}."},{"line_end":352,"line_start":352,"support":"Text states for chi=0.2, 0.25, 0.3, perturbation theory predicts xi ~ W^{-4}."}],"condition_id":"stealthiness_chi_0_2","statement":"Stealthiness parameter chi = 0.2, corresponding to k0 = 2*pi*chi = 0.4*pi. For this chi, the first-order term vanishes because S(2k)=0, and the second-order term is the leading contribution."},{"anchors":[{"line_end":345,"line_start":345,"support":"Caption of Fig. loclength_W states eigenstates at energy E = -1.8."}],"condition_id":"energy_E_minus_1_8","statement":"Energy fixed at E = -1.8 for the numerical localization length measurements, corresponding to the horizontal red line in the phase diagram."},{"anchors":[{"line_end":138,"line_start":138,"support":"Text states 'We have set the hopping amplitude to unity, so that the strength of the disorder is the only relevant energy scale in the problem.'"}],"condition_id":"hopping_amplitude_unity","statement":"The hopping amplitude is set to unity, so the disorder strength W is the only relevant energy scale."}],"mechanisms":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":260,"line_start":260,"support":"Equation (eq:first-order) states 1/xi(k) = W^2 S(2k)/(8 sin^2 k)."},{"line_end":352,"line_start":352,"support":"Text states for chi=0.1, perturbation theory predicts xi ~ W^{-2}."}],"equations":["1/xi(k) = W^2 S(2k)/(8 sin^2 k)"],"mechanism_id":"first_order_self_energy","parameters":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":260,"line_start":260,"support":"Equation (eq:first-order) gives 1/xi = W^2 S(2k)/(8 sin^2 k), so the leading W-dependence is W^2, hence xi ~ W^{-2}."},{"line_end":352,"line_start":352,"support":"Text states for chi=0.1, perturbation theory predicts xi ~ W^{-2}."}],"name":"first_order_exponent","role":"exponent of W in the first-order inverse localization length, giving xi ~ W^{-2}","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":-2.0}],"statement":"First-order perturbative self-energy contribution to the inverse localization length for stealthy hyperuniform disorder, valid when S(2k) is nonzero. The leading term is 1/xi = W^2 S(2k)/(8 sin^2 k), giving xi ~ W^{-2}.","variable_names":["xi","W","k","S","k0","chi"]},{"anchors":[{"line_end":534,"line_start":526,"support":"Equation (eq:second-order) gives the full second-order inverse localization length expression with W^4 prefactor."},{"line_end":280,"line_start":280,"support":"Text states the correction to xi at second order will be of the form xi ~ W^{-4}."}],"equations":["1/xi(k) = W^4/(32 pi sin^2(k)) * [ ... ]"],"mechanism_id":"second_order_self_energy","parameters":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":280,"line_start":280,"support":"Text states the correction to xi at second order will be of the form xi ~ W^{-4}."},{"line_end":352,"line_start":352,"support":"Text states for chi=0.2, 0.25, 0.3, perturbation theory predicts xi ~ W^{-4}."}],"name":"second_order_exponent","role":"exponent of W in the second-order inverse localization length, giving xi ~ W^{-4}","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":-4.0}],"statement":"Second-order perturbative self-energy contribution to the inverse localization length, valid when the first-order term vanishes (2k < k0 < pi - k). The leading term is 1/xi = W^4 times a k,k0-dependent bracket divided by 32 pi sin^2(k), giving xi ~ W^{-4}.","variable_names":["xi","W","k","k0","chi"]}],"paper_scope":"The paper studies the 1D Anderson model with stealthy hyperuniform disorder, computing the localization length via a perturbative self-energy expansion and numerical diagonalization. It focuses on the scaling of the localization length with disorder strength W and stealthiness parameter chi, showing effective delocalization for finite systems. The selected cluster is the leading-order asymptotic scaling family for the normalized localization-length predictor.","readouts":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":316,"line_start":312,"support":"The inverse localization length is expressed as an asymptotic series in powers of W^2, supporting the scaling form."},{"line_end":352,"line_start":352,"support":"Text states perturbation theory predicts xi ~ W^{-2} for chi=0.1 and xi ~ W^{-4} for chi=0.2, 0.25, 0.3."}],"definition":"Theoretical leading asymptotic normalized predictor Y(q) = lim_{W_ref->0 at fixed q} xi(q*W_ref)/xi(W_ref) = q^{-p}, where p is the leading-order exponent. This is a deterministic leading-order prediction, not a finite-size numerical xi.","name":"normalized_localization_length_predictor","resolution":null,"statistic":"Theoretical leading asymptotic normalized predictor; no numerical averaging or fitting is involved.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless"}],"source":{"arxiv_versioned_id":"2509.13502v3","citation":"Carlo Vanoni, Effective Delocalization in the One-Dimensional Anderson Model with Stealthy Disorder, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/nl7n-bqn4","journal_doi":"10.1103/nl7n-bqn4"},"source_ambiguities":[]}
